<DocsLayout title="Multi-Tenancy">
  <h1>Multi-Tenancy</h1>
  <p>Identity itself is single-tenant per deployment: one container, one product, one tenant ID. Multi-tenancy is provided by the data plane &mdash; <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/tenant-shard-db">EntDB</a> &mdash; which gives every tenant its own per-tenant SQLite database file.</p>

  <h2>The deployment unit</h2>
  <p>Each product is its own Identity deployment with its own tenant ID:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash"># Product A
docker run -d \
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=product-a \
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=entdb:50051 \
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=app.product-a.com \
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=https://app.product-a.com \
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:latest

# Product B — same EntDB cluster, different tenant
docker run -d \
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=product-b \
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=entdb:50051 \
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=app.product-b.com \
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=https://app.product-b.com \
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:latest</code></pre>

  <Callout type="info" title="Why one container per product?">
    Passkey relying-party IDs, OAuth client IDs, TOTP issuer names, and CORS origins are all per-product configuration. Trying to multiplex multiple products inside one Identity instance would require all of those to become per-request, which adds attack surface for marginal gain. Containers are cheap.
  </Callout>

  <h2>How EntDB delivers isolation</h2>
  <p>Every Identity operation passes <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code> to EntDB. EntDB shards data per tenant:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each tenant gets a separate SQLite database file</li>
    <li>No cross-tenant queries; the tenant ID is part of every read and write</li>
    <li>Optional per-tenant encryption (SQLCipher) lets you crypto-shred a tenant by destroying its data encryption key</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Within a tenant, Identity uses these EntDB type IDs:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>type_id</th><th>Node</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>User</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Group</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>RefreshToken</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Passkey</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>TOTPSecret</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>AuditEvent</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Other services running against the same EntDB cluster must use type IDs <code>100+</code> to avoid collisions with Identity's reserved range.</p>

  <h2>The tenant claim in JWTs</h2>
  <p>Every access token Identity issues carries a <code>tenant</code> claim equal to <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code>. Downstream services should verify both the signature and that the <code>tenant</code> claim matches their expected tenant before honoring the token.</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-json">{
  "sub": "user_01HX...",
  "email": "alice@product-a.com",
  "tenant": "product-a",
  "role": "member",
  "iat": 1714750000,
  "exp": 1714750900
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Sharing an EntDB cluster between products</h2>
  <p>Multiple Identity deployments can point at the same EntDB instance &mdash; the tenant ID keeps user pools fully separate. This is the recommended deployment model for an organisation running several products: one shared EntDB cluster, one Identity container per product.</p>

  <h2>Sharing JWKS between services within a tenant</h2>
  <p>All services for a given product fetch JWKS from the same Identity deployment. SSO between two apps in the same product works out of the box: both verify against the same JWKS URL and accept the same <code>tenant</code> claim. See <a href="/identity/docs/examples/multi-app-sso">Multi-App SSO</a> for a worked example.</p>
</DocsLayout>
